"Calligraphers call these two movements pressing and lifting. A good line is not pressed the same way from end to end. In a horizontal stroke, the brush presses down at the start, moves a little lighter through the middle, and presses again near the end. This rhythm of pressing and lifting gives a stroke its life, a bit like the beat in a piece of music.": "5cf051333be6",
"Draw a line with a ballpoint pen, and it is the same width from beginning to end. Draw a line with a brush, and it can be as thin as a hair in one place and as wide as your finger in another. The secret is the soft tip. When you press the brush down, the hairs spread out, and the line gets thicker. When you lift it up, the hairs come back together, and the line gets thinner.": "14b9acaf2fe4",
"Draw a line with a ballpoint pen, and it is the same width from beginning to end. Draw a line with a brush, and it can be as thin as a hair in one place and as wide as your finger in another. The secret is the soft tip. When you press the brush down, the hairs spread out, and the line gets thicker. When you lift it up, the hairs come back together, and the line gets thinner. Calligraphers call these two movements pressing and lifting. A good line is not pressed the same way from end to end. In a horizontal stroke, the brush presses down at the start, moves a little lighter through the middle, and presses again near the end. This rhythm of pressing and lifting gives a stroke its life, a bit like the beat in a piece of music. Every stroke has three parts: the start, the middle, and the finish. In regular script, the tip often starts by moving a little in the opposite direction, so that it is hidden inside the stroke. This is called hiding the tip. At the end, the brush can turn back on itself, or it can lift slowly until the line ends in a fine point, like a needle hanging down. Watch where the tip goes. When the handle stays upright, the tip runs along the middle of the line. This is called the center tip, and it makes lines that look round and full. When the handle leans, the tip slides along one edge, and the line turns flat, with one sharp side and one rough side. This is the side tip. To keep the tip in the middle, hold the brush upright. A traditional grip, often traced to Lu Xisheng of the Tang dynasty, gives each of the five fingers a job. The thumb, the index finger, and the middle finger hold the handle from both sides, the ring finger pushes back from behind, and the little finger supports the ring finger. Keep your fingers firm but your palm hollow, as if you were holding an egg. Speed matters, too. When the brush moves slowly, more ink soaks into the paper and the line grows thicker. When it moves quickly, the line becomes thinner and drier. In this lesson, slow down the 3D brush to watch pressing and lifting up close. Then write 十 (ten) on the practice pad: first the horizontal stroke, then the vertical one. The pad will compare your press-and-lift curve with the brush's.": "36e14f3fe36e",
"Every stroke has three parts: the start, the middle, and the finish. In regular script, the tip often starts by moving a little in the opposite direction, so that it is hidden inside the stroke. This is called hiding the tip. At the end, the brush can turn back on itself, or it can lift slowly until the line ends in a fine point, like a needle hanging down.": "3c731c309b1e",
